(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-cytopenias-hemochromatosis-vignettes"]={"title":"Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with poorly controlled rheumatoid arthritis has anemia of chronic disease: hemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL), ferritin 260 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL) and total iron-binding capacity 220 mcg/dL (normal 250-450 mcg/dL). Which step matters most for her anemia?","choices":["Hematology referral","Better control of the arthritis","Erythropoietin supplementation","Oral iron supplementation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treating the underlying condition is the most important treatment for anemia of chronic disease; her rheumatoid arthritis is poorly controlled, and its inflammation drives the hepcidin rise and the erythropoietin suppression behind her anemia. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 23, 26"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has iron deficiency anemia from heavy menstrual bleeding; workup finds no other source. Her hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL). She is starting oral iron and does not wish to become pregnant. Which added measure addresses the cause of her iron loss?","choices":["A continuous-cycle oral contraceptive","A parenteral iron infusion","Erythropoietin supplementation","Colonoscopy and endoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of iron deficiency starts by treating the underlying cause. For menstrual blood loss an oral contraceptive can be given, and a continuous-cycle contraceptive should be considered, while iron is replaced. Her loss is menstrual and she wants contraception. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with myelodysplastic syndrome has ring sideroblasts (iron-containing mitochondria ringing the nucleus) on his marrow and needs regular red cell transfusions; his hemoglobin before each one is about 7.6 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL). Which agent was recently approved for this setting?","choices":["Luspatercept","Epoetin alfa","Succimer","Ferrous sulfate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Luspatercept is a new agent recently approved for myelodysplastic syndrome with ring sideroblasts, his diagnosis. Myelodysplastic syndrome is the usual association of acquired sideroblastic anemia, and his transfusion support also warrants hematology involvement. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with sickle cell disease is reviewed in clinic. She asks which symptoms mean she should seek care urgently rather than waiting for her next hematology visit. Which instruction is correct?","choices":["Pale skin","Dark urine after fava beans","Fever or chest pain","Mild fatigue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Red flags in sickle cell disease are fever, chest pain or hypoxemia, and a new neurologic deficit, each requiring urgent assessment. Education includes vaccines, fever and chest pain precautions and hematology follow-up. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 93, 98, 107"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 79 fL (normal 80-100 fL), a dimorphic smear with microcytic, normal and macrocytic cells, serum iron 210 mcg/dL (normal 60-170 mcg/dL), transferrin saturation 62% (normal 20-50%) and ferritin 540 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL). Which test shows the defining feature of her likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bone marrow aspirate","Fecal occult blood test","Blood lead level","C-reactive protein level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A dimorphic smear with excess total body iron (high serum iron, transferrin saturation and ferritin) suggests sideroblastic anemia. Its defining feature is ring sideroblasts on a bone marrow aspirate: iron-containing mitochondria form a basophilic ring around the nucleus. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a hemoglobin of 10.4 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), a white cell count of 7,200 per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000) and platelets of 240,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000), with a mean corpuscular volume of 88 fL (normal 80-100 fL). Which value should be reviewed next to judge whether his bone marrow response is adequate?","choices":["Bone marrow aspirate","Reticulocyte index","Serum ferritin","Red cell distribution width"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The initial approach reviews the complete blood count across all three cell lines (his white cells and platelets are normal, so there is no pancytopenia), evaluates the mean corpuscular volume (88 fL, normal), then reviews the reticulocyte index to determine whether the bone marrow response is adequate. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a white blood cell count of 2,800 cells per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). His differential shows neutrophils 32% (normal 40-70%), bands 4% (normal 0-5%), lymphocytes 56% and monocytes 8%. How is his neutropenia classified?","choices":["Not neutropenic","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Absolute neutrophil count = 2,800 x (32 + 4) / 100 = 1,008 cells per microliter, inside the mild range of 1,000-1,500. Using the neutrophils alone would give 896 and wrongly call it moderate. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with autoimmune hemolytic anemia has gone into remission on treatment. She asks what her follow-up will involve. Which follow-up plan is appropriate?","choices":["Monitor relapse and clots","Avoid oxidative triggers","Genetic counseling","Discharge; cure is certain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Follow-up of autoimmune hemolysis is to treat the cause, monitor for relapse and thrombosis and keep hematology care. Relapse may occur, and the outcome depends on the cause and the response to treatment. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 73, 107"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy has a white blood cell count of 1,800 cells per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). Her differential shows neutrophils 20% (normal 40-70%), bands 5% (normal 0-5%), lymphocytes 65% and monocytes 10%. What is her absolute neutrophil count?","choices":["90 cells per microliter","360 cells per microliter","450 cells per microliter","1,170 cells per microliter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absolute neutrophil count = 1,800 x (20 + 5) / 100 = 450 cells per microliter, which is severe neutropenia (below 500). If she develops fever, that count makes it an emergency needing cultures and empiric antipseudomonal antibiotics. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 59, 60"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has beta thalassemia with moderate chronic anemia, splenomegaly and jaundice. Her hemoglobin is stable at 8.5 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL), and she has no symptoms requiring urgent transfusion. Which supportive treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Glucocorticoids","Scheduled transfusions","Folic acid","Oral iron"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of thalassemia is supportive: transfusion only when absolutely necessary, because it can exacerbate iron overload, plus folic acid and genetic counseling. A stable girl without an urgent need gets the supportive measures. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 72, 74, 87, 89"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man on hemodialysis for kidney failure has iron deficiency from the iron lost at each treatment; his hemoglobin is 10.3 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL). Which iron option is designed to replace the iron lost during each hemodialysis session?","choices":["Ferrous sulfate on alternate days","Oral pyridoxine supplementation","Succimer chelation therapy","Ferric pyrophosphate citrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ferric pyrophosphate citrate is an approved additive to the dialysate, designed to replace the iron that is lost during each hemodialysis treatment. It delivers iron through the dialysis circuit itself. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency develops a urinary tract infection while traveling. He has a supply of trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole from a previous prescription and asks whether he may take it. What is the best response?","choices":["Safe unless he is female","Avoid this antibiotic","Take it with extra fluids","Take a half course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is on the list of common triggers of acute hemolytic anemia in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, with nitrofurantoin, quinolones and phenazopyridine. His infection needs an antibiotic that is not one of these oxidant triggers. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 76, 79"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has autoimmune hemolytic anemia with a positive direct antiglobulin test. He is stable with hemoglobin 8.6 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), and secondary causes have been ruled out. Which is the initial treatment?","choices":["Hydroxyurea","Glucocorticoids","Rituximab","Splenectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With secondary causes ruled out and no instability, autoimmune hemolytic anemia is treated with glucocorticoids initially. Rituximab is the other named agent, and immunosuppressants or splenectomy are further options. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has fatigue, dark urine and yellow eyes. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL), reticulocytes 6% (normal 0.5-2.5%), haptoglobin undetectable (normal 30-200 mg/dL), lactate dehydrogenase 610 U/L (normal 140-280 U/L), and the urinalysis shows hemoglobin. Which pattern of red cell destruction do these results indicate?","choices":["Marrow production failure","Iron deficiency anemia","Biliary obstruction","Intravascular hemolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In intravascular hemolysis free hemoglobin is picked up by haptoglobin and carried to the liver, so haptoglobin becomes decreased or absent, and because that binding is incomplete some hemoglobin reaches the urine. Her undetectable haptoglobin and urine hemoglobin fit, with a high reticulocyte count and lactate dehydrogenase from cell lysis. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 66, 67, 69"},{"q":"A 5-month-old boy who missed newborn screening is brought in with poor weight gain, pallor and painful swelling of both hands and feet. Hemoglobin analysis confirms sickle cell disease. His mother asks why he was well until now. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Fetal hemoglobin is being replaced","The disease begins at puberty","An infection caused the disease","Breast milk protected him"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infants who bypass newborn screening usually present within the first two years of life, and symptoms usually start around 5 months with the transition from fetal hemoglobin (HbF) to HbS. Swelling of the extremities comes from vaso-occlusion, with failure to thrive, pain, anemia and splenomegaly. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 92, 97"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia and gastroesophageal reflux takes metformin, amlodipine, atorvastatin and omeprazole. She has a mean corpuscular volume of 107 fL (normal 80-100 fL), hypersegmented neutrophils, a low folate level and a normal B12 level. Which of her medications is listed as a cause of folate deficiency?","choices":["Omeprazole","Amlodipine","Atorvastatin","Metformin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metformin is on the list of drugs that cause folate deficiency, with methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil, hydroxyurea and phenytoin. Her low folate with a megaloblastic picture and a normal B12 fits that drug cause. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 10, 39, 42"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has taken oral iron for iron deficiency anemia for 2 months. Her hemoglobin is now 13.2 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and her mean corpuscular volume 86 fL (normal 80-100 fL). She asks whether she can stop the iron. What should she be told?","choices":["Stop now that values are normal","Continue for 2 more weeks","Continue for the rest of her life","Continue for 3-6 more months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her values are back to normal at 2 months, the expected timing. Iron therapy should continue for 3-6 months after normal hematologic values are restored, to replenish iron stores. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has 2 weeks of fatigue and jaundice. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (normal 12.0-16.0 g/dL), reticulocytes 8% (normal 0.5-2.5%), and haptoglobin is low (normal 30-200 mg/dL). Which test will show whether her red cells are coated with IgG or complement?","choices":["Direct antiglobulin test","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Glucose-6-phosphate level","Iron panel with ferritin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lupus is a common secondary cause of autoimmune hemolytic anemia. In the direct antiglobulin, or Coombs, test her washed red cells are combined with Coombs reagent, which binds only cells coated with IgG or complement; a positive test cross-links and agglutinates them. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 71, 72"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 88 fL (normal 80-100 fL), reticulocyte count low, ferritin 280 ng/mL (normal 30-300 ng/mL) and transferrin saturation 14% (normal 20-50%). Which treatment step does his iron panel call for?","choices":["No iron, since ferritin is normal","Folic acid supplementation","Bone marrow biopsy","Correct the iron deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In anemia of chronic kidney disease a low transferrin saturation may indicate iron-restricted erythropoiesis despite a normal or high ferritin, because inflammation and hepcidin restrict available iron. The treatment step is to correct the iron deficiency, with oral or intravenous iron, and to consider an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent by stage, hemoglobin and risk. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 52, 53"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, lisinopril for hypertension, atorvastatin for high cholesterol and omeprazole for reflux. His hemoglobin is 9.5 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), and a bone marrow aspirate shows ring sideroblasts (iron-containing mitochondria forming a ring around the nucleus). Which medication should be stopped?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Isoniazid","Omeprazole","Lisinopril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ring sideroblasts define sideroblastic anemia, and isoniazid is one of the drugs behind its acquired, reversible type (with chlorambucil, alcohol, copper deficiency, hypothermia and lead). Treatment begins by removing the offending agent. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 29, 32, 33"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has fatigue and his brother has hemochromatosis. His iron panel shows ferritin 180 micrograms/L (normal 12-300 micrograms/L) and transferrin saturation 32% (normal 20-50%), and he has no infection or inflammatory illness. How should this panel be classified?","choices":["Iron overload present","Secondary hemochromatosis","Iron deficiency","No iron overload"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If ferritin is less than 300 micrograms/L and transferrin saturation is less than 45 percent, there is no iron overload. His ferritin of 180 and saturation of 32 percent are both below the cut-offs, and with no inflammatory illness the acute phase caution does not apply. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with B12 deficiency had months of numbness in her hands and feet and an unsteady gait before she was diagnosed. She has now started B12 replacement. She asks whether her numbness will go away. Which response is accurate?","choices":["It may or may not improve","It will resolve within weeks","It will improve once folate is added","It will worsen on B12"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neurologic findings of B12 deficiency may or may not improve with B12 supplementation; the blood counts improve, but delayed B12 treatment may leave deficits, so her neurologic recovery is monitored. Her months of untreated symptoms make a lasting deficit possible. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 45, 49, 106"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease, on several medications, is found to have a new anemia on routine labs. Which approach is listed for elderly patients with a cytopenia?","choices":["Newborn screen review","Treat fully regardless of goals","Check iron, B12, drugs, marrow","Treat as heavy menses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the elderly, check iron and B12 deficiency, chronic kidney disease, drugs and marrow disorders such as myelodysplastic syndrome, then tailor treatment to function, comorbidities and goals. Her kidney disease and medication list are both on that checklist. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy with known glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency develops abdominal pain, jaundice and dark urine 2 days after he was found playing in a closet where mothballs were stored. Which exposure is the likely trigger?","choices":["Fava beans","Naphthalene","Lead","Copper"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Naphthalene in mothballs is a listed trigger of acute hemolytic anemia in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. The 2-day lag, abdominal pain, jaundice and dark urine match drug- or toxin-induced hemolysis after oxidative stress. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 79, 81"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman started ferrous sulfate two weeks ago for iron deficiency anemia (hemoglobin 10.4 g/dL, normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and now has hard, infrequent stools. She wants to keep taking the iron. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to parenteral iron now","Stop iron until stools normalize","Add a laxative or stool softener","Take it with an antacid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal side effects of ferrous sulfate are common, nausea and especially constipation, so it is given with at least an as-needed laxative or stool softener. She wants to continue, and treating the constipation keeps her adherent. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a hemoglobin of 9.9 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 73 fL (normal 80-100 fL), ferritin 7 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL), total iron-binding capacity 470 mcg/dL (normal 250-450 mcg/dL) and transferrin saturation 6% (normal 20-50%). She has no inflammatory illness. Which anemia do these iron studies fit?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Sideroblastic anemia","Thalassemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her iron panel is the iron deficiency pattern: reduced ferritin (7 ng/mL), reduced transferrin saturation (6%) and increased total iron-binding capacity (470 mcg/dL), with microcytosis (mean corpuscular volume below 80 fL). With no inflammation, a low ferritin is reliable. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 15, 27"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with inflammatory bowel disease has a hemoglobin of 10.0 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume 76 fL (normal 80-100 fL) and a C-reactive protein of 4.8 mg/dL (normal below 1.0 mg/dL). His ferritin is 21 ng/mL (normal 15-200 ng/mL). How should his iron status be classified?","choices":["Iron overload from inflammation","Chronic disease plus iron deficiency","Chronic disease alone; ferritin is normal","Adequate iron stores for inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In anemia of chronic disease serum ferritin should be normal or increased; a value below 30 ng/mL indicates coexistent iron deficiency. His ferritin of 21 ng/mL sits inside the reference range yet below 30 ng/mL, despite active inflammation that would push ferritin up. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy with sickle cell disease suddenly develops weakness of his right arm and slurred speech at school. He is afebrile. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Hematology visit next month","Start penicillin prophylaxis","Urgent assessment","Oral hydration at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A new neurologic deficit is a red flag in sickle cell disease requiring urgent assessment, as are fever and chest pain with hypoxemia. Stroke is a pediatric complication, about 300 times more common than in unaffected children. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 93, 98"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has fatigue, recurrent infections and easy bruising. Her complete blood count shows pancytopenia, the reticulocyte count is low, and the peripheral smear shows no abnormal cells. Which test is required to make the diagnosis?","choices":["Repeat complete blood count","Methylmalonic acid level","Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies","Bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pancytopenia in a young woman with infections and bruising raises aplastic anemia, and a bone marrow biopsy is required for the diagnosis and to exclude other causes. Urgent or emergent consultation with a hematologist/oncologist is indicated. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 48, 55, 58"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has 1 week of fatigue and mild scleral icterus. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) with reticulocytes 5% (normal 0.5-2.5%), and hemolytic anemia is suspected. Which bilirubin result is expected?","choices":["Normal total bilirubin","Low unconjugated fraction","Unconjugated above conjugated","Conjugated above unconjugated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hemolytic anemia both bilirubin fractions are likely elevated, with unconjugated greater than conjugated, because macrophages destroying red cells release unconjugated bilirubin that the liver conjugates afterward. His icterus and high reticulocytes fit a hemolytic source upstream of the liver. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 66, 67"}]};
